      "message": "* File: /PATCHSET_LEVEL (Line FILE)\nUser1:\nHow do we expect this to be used on gerrit-review to solve the comment spam problem? Most importantly:\n\nWho are we granting the Write Change perm to?\n\nHow does someone request to be granted that permission?\n\nUser1:\nCopying over notes from the [Google Doc](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1GCioZymU01gacga2PKk3olM4KHvSVpeZyHVQ2b7NwUQ/edit?tab\u003dt.0) about the workflow UX challenges we should try to solve in this change (or immediate follow-ups):\n\n* Users need to *know* that the project is SPAM-protected and where/how to ask (or who?) for permission to post comments\n* It looks like the SPAM-protected workflow risks being a bad user experience vs. the current workflow\n* We currently have a similar issue for uploading changes (users cannot upload and wonder why?)\n* Can we just resolve it with a nice error message? This should be project-specific (perhaps a text field in the project settings), as different projects may have different policies for preventing SPAMers.\n* What could a default workflow look like?\n  - Provide a “button” to request to be unblocked and be able to comment\n  - The “button” sends an e-mail to someone and they add you to a group\n  - Possible enhanced/plugin version: Pressing the button triggers a “CAPTCHA” to demonstrate you are a human and then you’re added to the group automatically (project owner configures which group in the project settings?)\n  - Other possible enhanced version: Pressing the button triggers a “request to be added to group” workflow (new Gerrit feature) and the project/group owner approves the request\n\nUser2:\nThanks Nasser. I agree that contributors who are blocked need to understand the restriction and how to request access.\n\nFor this series, though, I\u0027d like to keep the scope focused on the permission and its enforcement.\n\nThe change already settles who and how: Write Change is a ref permission granted by project owners via the Access screen, with the usual change-owner / branch-owner / project-owner / admin exemptions.\n\nI suggest tracking discoverability, access requests, and improved denial messaging together in Issue 391666234. That would let us design an actionable experience rather than address individual pieces in this enforcement change.\n\nCould we keep this series focused on:\n\n- change 648203: configuration\n- change 648204: permission\n- change 648205: enforcement\n\nand take that UI/UX work forward separately?\n\nUser3:\n\u003e take that UI/UX work forward separately?\n\nI think it makes sense. As long as we have a minimal feedback message we can then iterate on it, as discussed during the maintainers catchup about this issue.",
